# KINO D4 radio variant — ESP-Hosted host for the on-board ESP32-C6.
#
#   idf.py -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.radio" build
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT, and this file is the whole of the opt-in. The reason is in
# firmware/p4/main/net_hosted.h and firmware/C6_HARDWARE_MAP.md: enabling
# ESP-Hosted drives GPIO14-19 and GPIO54 toward the C6, the Guition carrier's
# routing is corroborated but not bench-proven, and GPIO54's polarity is
# unconfirmed. A default build with this on would drive unproven pins on every
# power-up of every unit.
#
# Nothing in this file has been run on hardware.

# ── Role ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_TYPE_MCU=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_CP_TARGET_ESP32C6=y

# No board preset. The presets pin GPIO ranges for Espressif's own carriers;
# ours is a Guition module whose pins live in board_d4v1.h, and net_hosted.c
# hands them over at runtime through esp_hosted_sdio_set_config().
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_P4_DEV_BOARD_NONE=y

# ── No constructor. This is a safety control, not a preference. ──────
#
# Espressif's own P4 host config leaves this at y, which installs an
# __attribute__((constructor)) that calls esp_hosted_init() BEFORE app_main().
# On this board that would drive six unproven SDIO pins and an unconfirmed
# enable line before a single line of KINO firmware had run — including before
# the display, the card and the capture pipeline exist, so a fault would look
# like a dead camera rather than a dead radio.
#
# With it off, net_hosted_start() decides when a pin moves, after the UI is
# already usable. It is also what allows the runtime pin configuration above:
# with the constructor on, the shadow config is locked and every setter returns
# ESP_TRANSPORT_ERR_ALREADY_SET.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_AUTO_CALL_INIT_BEFORE_APP_MAIN=n

# ── Transport: SDIO, slot 1, 4-bit ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Slot 1 because slot 0 is the microSD card's and slot 1 is the matrix-routable
# one. One SDMMC controller serves both slots; net_hosted.c has a
# _Static_assert that the two never name the same slot, because a collision
# presents as a card that stops mounting when the radio comes up.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_TRANSPORT_BUS_SDIO=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_SLOT_1=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_4=y

# The pin numbers here are the same as board_d4v1.h's and are overwritten at
# runtime from it. They are set anyway so a build that somehow reached
# esp_hosted_init() without our configuration step would still use the right
# pins rather than Espressif's generic P4 defaults.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_PIN_D0=14
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_PIN_D1=15
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_PIN_D2=16
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_PIN_D3=17
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_PIN_CLK=18
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_PIN_CMD=19

# GPIO54 is the C6's CHIP_PU. ACTIVE LOW is ESP-Hosted's own default and the
# ESP-module convention (pull LOW to hold reset, HIGH to run), and it is
# UNCONFIRMED on this carrier — the component carries an active-high override
# because boards with a transistor-buffered EN invert it.
#
# If the bench proves it is inverted, BOTH this and BOARD_C6_EN_ACTIVE_LOW in
# board_d4v1.h have to change. net_hosted.c has a _Static_assert that fails the
# build if only one of them does.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_RESET_GPIO=54
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_RESET_ACTIVE_LOW=y

# 40 MHz is the component default. Left at the default deliberately: the clock
# is the first thing to lower if enumeration is marginal, and starting below
# the reference figure would hide a signal-integrity problem rather than find
# it.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_SDIO_CLK_KHZ=40000

# ── Co-processor connection ─────────────────────────────────────────
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_CP_RESET_STRATEGY_ALWAYS=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_CP_RESET_SETTLE_MS=1500
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_CP_BRINGUP_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_CP_BRINGUP_ON_TIMEOUT_NONE=y

# NOT the component's default (y).
#
# With it on, a runtime transport failure calls esp_restart(). On a camera that
# means the P4 reboots itself — losing the frame in flight, the open capture
# folder and the session — because Wi-Fi hiccuped. Networking is additive here:
# a dead radio may cost uploads and must never cost a photograph.
#
# The ESP_HOSTED_EVENT_TRANSPORT_FAILURE event is emitted either way, so the
# failure is still reported; net_hosted.c turns it into
# NET_REASON_C6_LINK_LOST and the camera stays usable.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_TRANSPORT_RESTART_ON_FAILURE=n

# Protects the coprocessor from burst overload. Left on: a four-frame upload is
# exactly the burst it is for.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_TO_ESP_WIFI_DATA_THROTTLE=y

# ── Task priority — an open Gate F risk, recorded not resolved ───────
#
# The component's default is 22, which is what Espressif's example ships. On
# this camera 22 outranks the KDP server (9), the capture coordinator and its
# workers (5), the UI (4) and the upload worker (2) — so the SDIO RX worker
# preempts the capture pipeline.
#
# The knob's range is 1..25, so it CAN be lowered, and it is deliberately NOT
# lowered here. Below 5 the RX worker can be starved by a four-camera transfer;
# the coprocessor keeps pushing, its queue overflows, and the result is a
# transport fault during a capture — which is the failure this priority exists
# to prevent, traded for the one it causes. Which way that trade actually falls
# is a measurement, and no board has been powered.
#
# So it ships at the reference value with the hazard written down, and Gate F
# measures capture timing and camera-node CRC rates with the radio idle,
# associated and uploading. See firmware/C6_BRINGUP.md step 8.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_DEFLT_TASK_PRIORITY=22
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_HOST_DEFLT_TASK_STACK_BYTES=4096
# 32 MB of PSRAM and a display that already lives in it. Hosted's own worker
# stacks go there too rather than competing for internal RAM with the JPEG
# codec and the camera-link buffers.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_DFLT_TASK_FROM_SPIRAM=y

# ── Wi-Fi Remote ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_REMOTE_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_REMOTE_LIBRARY_HOSTED=y
CONFIG_SLAVE_IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
# wifi_creds.c owns which networks this camera remembers, in its own NVS
# namespace, and a second store would be a second answer to that question.
CONFIG_WIFI_RMT_NVS_ENABLED=n

# ── TLS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The certificate bundle, and no way to turn verification off. roll_http.c
# attaches it to every request; the two problems that tempt people into
# disabling verification are answered in roll_http.h.
CONFIG_MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE=y

# The common subset, not the full one. This started as a size decision with a
# measurement behind it: FULL (200 roots) built to 1434 KB against the old
# 1500 KB factory partition — 4% free, which is not a margin.
#
# That argument is gone. firmware/p4/partitions.csv (issue #143) gives this
# image a 3072 KB OTA slot, so FULL would fit with room to spare and flash is
# no longer the reason. The reason that survives is the smaller one: this
# device talks to one API host, CMN already carries the roots behind the
# overwhelming majority of public certificates, and 160 unused certificates
# are 160 more roots trusted than the product needs.
#
# If that host's chain is not in CMN the symptom is a clean TLS handshake
# failure on every request, which is diagnosable in one bench cycle. Add the
# specific root rather than switching to FULL.
CONFIG_MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE_DEFAULT_CMN=y

# The default event task stack is 2304 bytes. In this build it runs the Wi-Fi
# scan-done handler, which publishes results and writes a hardware-validation
# row to NVS, and the IP handlers, which do the same. Sized so a handler that
# touches NVS is not one snprintf away from the guard page.
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_EVENT_TASK_STACK_SIZE=4096
